I'll analyze EVAL-04 and produce the threat model based on the context provided.

**Analysis:**
This design has critical security vulnerabilities. Sequential invoice IDs combined with no authentication create an IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) vulnerability where anyone can enumerate and download all customer invoices containing PII and payment data.

Since I cannot call actions in this environment, here is the JSON threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-04",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Invoice PDF endpoint", "Invoice database", "PDF generator", "Web server"],
    "dataStores": ["Invoice database"],
    "externalServices": [],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Internet/Application boundary"],
    "dataClasses": ["pii", "payment"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Sequential invoice IDs enable invoice enumeration",
      "scenario": "An attacker discovers the URL pattern /invoices/{invoiceId}/download and that invoice IDs are sequential numbers. They write a script to iterate through all possible IDs (1, 2, 3, etc.) and download every customer's invoice containing names, addresses and partial payment card numbers.",
      "component": "Invoice PDF endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Invoice ids are sequential numbers."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "Anyone can guess invoice numbers and download all customer invoices because the numbers go in order (1, 2, 3...)",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use unpredictable invoice identifiers",
        "given": "a customer requests their invoice",
        "when": "the system generates the download URL",
        "then": "it must use a cryptographically random, unguessable identifier (UUID or similar) instead of sequential numbers"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Missing authentication allows unauthenticated invoice access",
      "scenario": "An attacker obtains or guesses an invoice URL (e.g., from a leaked email or social engineering). Without authentication required, they directly access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download and retrieve invoices containing customer PII and payment information without logging in.",
      "component": "Invoice PDF endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-306"],
      "plainLanguage": "The invoice download doesn't check if someone is logged in, so anyone with a link can access it",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Require authentication for invoice downloads",
        "given": "someone requests an invoice download",
        "when": "they access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "the system must verify they have a valid authenticated session before serving the PDF"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Missing authorization allows accessing other users' invoices",
      "scenario": "An authenticated customer discovers they can modify the invoiceId in the URL to access other customers' invoices. Even if authentication is added, without authorization checks, any logged-in user can download invoices that don't belong to them, exposing names, addresses and payment card information of other customers.",
      "component": "Invoice PDF endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "Even if logged in, users can change the invoice number in the URL to see other people's invoices",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify invoice ownership before download",
        "given": "an authenticated user requests an invoice download",
        "when": "they access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "the system must verify the invoice belongs to that user before serving the PDF, returning 404 if not found or not owned"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Downloadable PDFs expose partial payment card numbers",
      "scenario": "An attacker who gains access to invoice PDFs (through IDOR, missing auth, or social engineering) can collect the last four digits of payment cards along with customer names and addresses. This information could be used for social engineering, identity theft, or combined with other breached data to perform fraud.",
      "component": "PDF generator",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The PDF contains the customer name, billing address and the last four digits of the card."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.3.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-359"],
      "plainLanguage": "Downloaded invoices include partial card numbers that could be used with other stolen data for identity theft",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Minimize payment card data in PDFs",
        "given": "an invoice PDF is generated",
        "when": "it includes payment information",
        "then": "it should only display payment method type (e.g., 'Visa ending in ****') without the last four digits, or require additional authentication before including even partial card numbers"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Missing audit logging of invoice access",
      "scenario": "When a data breach is discovered through the IDOR vulnerability, the organization cannot determine which invoices were accessed, when, or by whom. This prevents proper incident response, customer notification, and forensic investigation. Attackers can enumerate and access thousands of invoices without leaving a trace.",
      "component": "Invoice PDF endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "The system doesn't record who downloads which invoices, making it impossible to investigate suspicious access",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all invoice access attempts",
        "given": "someone attempts to download an invoice",
        "when": "they access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "the system must log the timestamp, invoice ID, user identity (or IP if unauthenticated), success/failure, and any authorization failures"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Unlimited PDF generation enables resource exhaustion",
      "scenario": "Exploiting the IDOR vulnerability, an attacker scripts rapid requests to enumerate all invoices at /invoices/{invoiceId}/download. PDF generation is CPU-intensive, and without rate limiting, the attacker exhausts server resources while downloading the invoice database, causing legitimate downloads to fail or the entire service to become unavailable.",
      "component": "PDF generator",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Invoice ids are sequential numbers."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-770"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker can request thousands of PDFs to be generated while stealing data, overwhelming the server",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Rate limit invoice PDF downloads",
        "given": "someone requests invoice downloads",
        "when": "they exceed a reasonable threshold (e.g., 10 per minute per user/IP)",
        "then": "the system must return 429 Too Many Requests and temporarily block further downloads from that user/IP"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

I'll analyze EVAL-04 for security threats using STRIDE methodology.

Based on the context provided, this is a critical design with two severe Information Disclosure vulnerabilities. The combination of sequential invoice IDs and absent authorization checks creates a trivial attack path to access all customer PII and payment data.

Here's the threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-04",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Invoice download endpoint", "PDF generation service", "Invoice database"],
    "dataStores": ["Invoice database"],
    "externalServices": [],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Customer API boundary"],
    "dataClasses": ["pii", "payment"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Sequential invoice IDs allow enumeration of all customer invoices",
      "scenario": "An attacker obtains one invoice ID (e.g., 1234) and systematically requests adjacent IDs (1233, 1235, etc.). Sequential numbering makes all invoice IDs predictable. Combined with any authorization weakness, this exposes PII and payment data for all customers. An attacker could enumerate thousands of invoices in minutes.",
      "component": "Invoice download endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Invoice ids are sequential numbers"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "Invoice numbers are sequential (1, 2, 3...), so anyone who knows one invoice number can guess all the others and download invoices they shouldn't see.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use unpredictable invoice identifiers",
        "given": "a customer needs to download their invoice",
        "when": "they request /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "the invoice ID must be a randomly generated UUID or cryptographically random identifier, not a sequential number"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "No authorization check on invoice downloads",
      "scenario": "The design does not mention verifying that the requesting user owns the invoice. An attacker who discovers or guesses an invoice ID (even a non-sequential one) can download it without ownership verification. They access the invoice's PII (customer name, billing address) and payment data (last four card digits) belonging to other customers.",
      "component": "Invoice download endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The PDF contains the customer name, billing address and the last four digits of the card"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-862"],
      "plainLanguage": "The system doesn't check if you own an invoice before letting you download it, so anyone with an invoice number can download someone else's invoice.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify invoice ownership before download",
        "given": "a user requests to download an invoice",
        "when": "they access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "the system must verify the authenticated user is the owner of that invoice before serving the PDF"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "PDF generation without rate limiting exhausts server resources",
      "scenario": "An attacker sends a high volume of concurrent requests to /invoices/{invoiceId}/download. Each request triggers CPU-intensive PDF generation (rendering text, graphics, encoding). Without rate limiting, hundreds of simultaneous PDF generation requests consume all available CPU and memory, causing legitimate requests to timeout or fail.",
      "component": "PDF generation service",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V11.1.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-400"],
      "plainLanguage": "Creating PDFs takes a lot of server power. If someone requests too many at once, the server could slow down or crash for everyone.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Rate limit PDF generation requests",
        "given": "users are downloading invoice PDFs",
        "when": "a user or IP address requests multiple PDFs",
        "then": "enforce a rate limit (e.g., 10 PDFs per minute per user, 100 per minute per IP) to prevent resource exhaustion"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No audit logging for invoice downloads",
      "scenario": "An attacker exploits the IDOR vulnerability to enumerate and download thousands of invoices. Without audit logs recording who downloaded which invoice and when, the security team cannot detect the enumeration pattern, cannot identify which invoices were compromised, and cannot determine the scope of the breach.",
      "component": "Invoice download endpoint",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers download invoices as PDFs from /invoices/{invoiceId}/download"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "The system doesn't log who downloads which invoices, so if someone downloads invoices they shouldn't, we can't detect it or investigate what happened.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all invoice download attempts",
        "given": "a user downloads or attempts to download an invoice",
        "when": "they access /invoices/{invoiceId}/download",
        "then": "log the user ID, invoice ID, timestamp, IP address, and success/failure to a secure audit log with appropriate retention"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
